# The composition map's null space has the dimension the layer graph says it has. One row.
#
# The identity
#   A part belongs to one fusion, so folding the parts onto their fusions gives fibre sizes whose
#   sum is the number of parts and whose box count is the rank of `F`. The layer graph counts the
#   same parts as edges. So `Σ parts = m` and `dim ker(F Phi) = Σ (parts - 1) = m - |fusions|`,
#   and neither side is free to move alone.
#   Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entry `composition_kernel`.
#
# Unconditional, and the forest form is the special case
#   Euler gives `m = n - c + dim(cycle)`, so on a graph whose cycle space is zero the identity
#   reads `dim ker = (n - c) - |fusions|`, which is the leaf count less the component count. That
#   is the form worth carrying in the head, and it is not the form asserted here: gating this law
#   on the cycle space would make it abstain exactly when the graph got interesting, and
#   `algebra/cycle_space.sqlc` is already the law that holds the forest.
#
# What it can actually catch, which is not the arithmetic
#   `Σ (parts - 1) = Σ parts - |fusions|` is true of any fold, so the content of this law is the
#   crossing: that the fold's `Σ parts` is the graph's `m` and the fold's box count is the graph's
#   rank contribution. `rank/graph_edges.sqlc` flattens a layer's `(filing, layer)` into one
#   string on a `/`, and `rank/graph_measures.sqlc` counts the edges `DISTINCT (graph, filing,
#   from_node, to_node)` afterwards. The flattening is injective on this corpus by luck rather
#   than by construction, and it is the one place that has to be true. Two layers colliding under
#   it, or one fusion naming one layer twice, drops `m` below `Σ parts` and this law is what
#   notices.
#
# The other three subspaces have their own laws
#   This one is the null space on the part side. `algebra/composition_row_space.sqlc` is the row
#   space beside it, `algebra/composition_image.sqlc` is the column space and the left null space
#   on the fusion side, and `algebra/composition_closure.sqlc` is what becomes of this dimension
#   once compositions nest. Four laws over one map, because it has four subspaces.
#
# Where the independent route is
#   Two relations reading one source by different code is the weaker half of the claim. The
#   stronger half is `examples/matrices/main.rs`, which builds `F` and `Phi` as dense matrices and
#   takes the rank by elimination, sharing nothing with either side of this law. A law and a
#   program disagreeing is the finding; this law alone would leave the dimension unchecked
#   against any arithmetic at all.
#
# Not vacuous by construction
#   A corpus with no fusions satisfies the identity as `0 = 0`. The floor requires parts, fusions
#   and edges before the comparison counts, so an empty corpus reports that it examined nothing
#   rather than that the model holds.
#
# Probe
#   examples/probes runs it as `composition_kernel`, by collapsing every part layer of one filing
#   onto the filing's own name in the composed SQL: `m` falls and `Σ parts` does not.
#   ⛔ Dropping the `DISTINCT` in `rank/graph_measures.sqlc` moves nothing instead, because this
#   corpus files no duplicate edge, so it is not a probe of anything.
-- rank/composition_kernel.sqlc folded, against rank/graph_measures.sqlc's layer row.
SELECT a.slug AS law, p.subject, p.holds, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(algebra/roster.sqlc)
) a
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT 'rank/composition_kernel' AS subject,
           x.parts > 0 AND x.fusions > 0 AND x.edges > 0
           AND x.parts = x.edges
           AND x.kernel = x.edges - x.fusions                                        AS holds,
           format('dim ker %s over %s part(s) in %s fusion(s); the layer graph has %s edge(s), '
                  '%s node(s), %s component(s) and cycle space %s',
                  x.kernel, x.parts, x.fusions, x.edges,
                  x.nodes, x.components, x.dim)                                      AS detail
    FROM (
        SELECT k.parts, k.fusions, k.kernel,
               m.m_edges AS edges, m.n_nodes AS nodes,
               m.c_components AS components, m.cycle_space_dim AS dim
        FROM      (
            SELECT coalesce(sum(c.parts), 0)      AS parts,
                   count(*)                       AS fusions,
                   coalesce(sum(c.kernel_dim), 0) AS kernel
            FROM ( :compose(rank/composition_kernel.sqlc) ) c
        ) k
        CROSS JOIN (
            :compose(rank/graph_measures.sqlc)
        ) m
        WHERE m.graph = 'layers' AND m.filing IS NULL
    ) x
) p ON true
WHERE a.slug = 'composition_kernel'
